          "content": "organisms and their surroundings. The study of this biological diversity is possible through a systematic framework that is provided by classification."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.263,
            0.131,
            0.588,
            0.149
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "India’s Scientific Contributions"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.239,
            0.153,
            0.867,
            0.249
          ],
          "content": "Some ancient Indian traditions, such as the Sangam Tinai classification of landscapes and the protection of sacred groves, demonstrate a sophisticated literary and cultural understanding of landscapes, and their biota. These customs effectively preserved locally diverse habitats, aligning with contemporary ecological principles, even if it is not articulated as a formal ecological theory."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.225,
            0.283,
            0.642,
            0.306
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "12.3 How to Classify Organisms?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.225,
            0.314,
            0.652,
            0.333
          ],
          "content": "**Activity 12.1: Let us compare and classify**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.224,
            0.335,
            0.888,
            0.372
          ],
          "content": "Fig. 12.2 shows the diversity of animals in an ecosystem. **Observe** the image carefully. Can you guess how the organisms are grouped in the image?"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.001,
            0.377,
            0.885,
            0.707
          ],
          "content": "",
